\subsection{Conventional heave baselines}
\label{sec:conventional-baselines}

Two conventional estimators receive the same measurement-only vertical
acceleration $a^{P}_{\uparrow}$ of \eqref{eq:proxy-vertical-acc} and, when
period-scaled, the same period estimate $\widehat{T}_z$ of
\eqref{eq:log-period}. Neither uses reference motion online.

\paragraph{Causal high-pass double integration (HPDI)}
Let $c_k$ be the normalized Butterworth coefficients of order $n$ and
$\omega_c$ the cutoff, with
\begin{equation}
  D(s)=\sum_{k=0}^{n} c_k\,\omega_c^{\,n-k} s^k,\qquad
  R(s)=\sum_{k=0}^{m-1} c_k\,\omega_c^{\,n-k} s^k .
\end{equation}
The heave estimate is
\begin{equation}
  \hat z(s)=\frac{1}{s^2}\Bigl[1-\frac{R(s)}{D(s)}\Bigr]\,a^{P}_{\uparrow}(s),
  \label{eq:hpdi}
\end{equation}
whose poles are those of $D$. The choice $m=n$ gives the classic cascaded
high-pass double integrator, for which $|G-1|\simeq c_{n-1}\,\omega_c/\omega$
above the corner. The choice $m<n$ gives a phase-compensated form with
$|G-1|=\mathcal{O}\bigl((\omega_c/\omega)^{\,n-m+1}\bigr)$, at the cost of gain
peaking near $\omega_c$. Any $m\ge 3$ yields zero steady-state heave for a
constant accelerometer bias and bounded variance for a random-walk bias. The
member $n=3$, $m=2$, $\omega_c=\omega_R$ coincides with the reduced
integral-regularizer response \eqref{eq:reduced-G}, so the family contains
OU--III's own scalar reduction. The cutoff is either fixed or
$\omega_c=2\pi\rho_c/\widehat{T}_z$, smoothed in log-frequency over
$0.75\,\widehat{T}_z$ and held for \SI{0.1}{s} between refreshes, as for the
OU--III schedule. Discretization uses the bilinear transform at the IMU rate.

\paragraph{Frequency-domain double integration (FDDI)}
Each record is mean-removed and reflect-padded by \SI{120}{s}. The estimate is
\begin{equation}
  \hat Z(f)=-\frac{W(f)}{(2\pi f)^2}\,A^{P}_{\uparrow}(f),
\end{equation}
where $W$ is a raised cosine rising from $f_1$ to $\kappa f_1$, and $f_1$ is
either fixed or $\rho_1/\operatorname{median}\widehat{T}_z$. FDDI is zero-phase
and non-causal, so it is reported as an offline reference rather than a
real-time competitor.

\paragraph{Selection protocol}
Parameters are selected on the development draws by minimizing the mean
normalized vertical RMS, then frozen. The search spaces are
$(n,m,\text{mode},\omega_c\text{ or }\rho_c)$ for HPDI, with
$3\le m\le n\le 6$, and $(\text{mode},f_1\text{ or }\rho_1,\kappa)$ for FDDI.
The best classic ($m=n$) and best compensated ($m<n$) HPDI are both reported.
Evaluation uses the ten paired seed triplets and the bootstrap of
Sec.~\ref{sec:paired-protocol}; Table~\ref{tab:conventional-baselines} gives the result.

